<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Stage</th><th>What runs</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td>the request arrives</td><td>each <code>middleware</code> rung the root layout files</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td>the route resolves</td><td>the <code>page.abide</code> at that path, inside its layouts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td>the render starts</td><td>every load the page reads, all of them at once</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td>the first bytes leave</td><td>the head, then the markup as far as it goes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5</td><td>the document closes</td><td>as soon as the walk is done</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6</td><td>the browser takes over</td><td>the same setup runs again, against the nodes already there</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7</td><td>the next navigation</td><td>the browser renders it, and shared layouts are not rebuilt</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The seven sections below are those seven rows, in that order and under the same names. Where
    each file in <code>src/</code> runs is a separate question, and the map for it is the last
    section.</p>

    <Section title="1. The request arrives">
        <p>The root <code>layout.abide</code>'s <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> registers the
        rungs every request passes before anything is routed. A rung there can answer an address of
        its own — a <code>llms.txt</code>, a webhook, anything you would rather answer without a
        page — and hand everything else on with <code>next()</code>.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="server" code={`<script server>
import { csp, middleware, onStart } from '@abide/abide/server'

middleware(csp(), requireSignIn())
onStart(async (start) => { await database.connect(); await start() })
middleware((next, { request }) =>
    new URL(request.url).pathname === '/llms.txt'
        ? new Response('# abide')
        : next(),
)
</script>

<slot/>`} />

        <p><em>1 file, 3 declarations</em> — a rung stack, a startup hook and an address of its own,
        in the one place an app owns before the router.</p>

        <p>Each <code>middleware</code> rung runs per request, and they run <strong>before</strong>
        the route resolves — so a rung has the request and nothing else. Anything that needs to know
        which row is being asked for is a handler's own rung instead, where the arguments exist.</p>

        <p>Everything abide serves goes through those rungs — <code>/__abide/**</code>, the built
        assets and <code>/__abide/health</code> included. A rung that refuses a caller with no
        credentials has to let the health check and the assets through by address. A
        <code>layout.abide</code> deeper in the tree files rungs of its own, which run after routing
        and over its section alone.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="2. The route resolves">
        <p>The path picks a <code>page.abide</code>, and the layouts in the directories above it
        wrap it.
        All of that renders into one document, and the root <code>src/layout.abide</code> writes
        it: written inside <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, its <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> is the
        document's head and its <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds the chrome and the slot every page
        goes in. The attributes on the two are the document's own, and live as on any element.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/layout.abide — excerpt" code={`<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
</head>
<body>
    <slot/>
</body>
</html>`} />

        <p>The bundle, and the CSS the client graph imported, are linked in for you — so a content
        hash never appears in anything you maintain. Write no <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> at all and
        abide serves its own minimal document: an encoding and a viewport.</p>

        <p>A page's <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> elements are merged into that document by key, deepest
        contributor winning, so a page's <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> replaces its layout's. The
        positions are known at compile time, which is why merging them costs step 4 no second walk
        of the body.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a>
        · <a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/render-a-document-yourself')}>Manual rendering</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="3. The render starts">
        <p>Rendering a page runs its setup: the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> blocks, the
        <code>reactive</code> calls, the bodies the markup reads. Each read that needs the server
        starts its work immediately, so the loads on a page are in flight together rather than in
        the order the markup happens to mention them.</p>

        <p>The total time is the slowest one, not the sum — and the first byte does not wait for any
        of them. The markup waits one turn, so a load that answers without waiting on anything is
        painted rather than left as a hole.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="4. The first bytes leave">
        <p>The head goes first, then the markup. A read of a value still in flight renders nothing
        and opens a <strong>sink</strong>, and the hole fills when the value lands. The markup after
        it keeps going out.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<script server>
export const getInvoice = GET(({ id }: { id: string }) =>
    database.invoice.find(id),
)
</script>

<script>
import { reactive, route } from '@abide/abide'

const invoice = reactive(() => getInvoice({ id: route.params.id }))
</script>

<h1>Invoice {invoice.number}</h1>
<p>{invoice.total} due {invoice.dueOn}</p>`} />

        <p>Three sinks, one document, no loading branch.</p>

        <p>There is no waiting form at all:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>The document</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{invoice.total}'}</code></td><td>flushes now, fills later</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#if invoice.ready()}'}</code></td><td>flushes now, with the <code>{'{:else}'}</code> to swap</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Neither of them waits, and there is no third row that does. The document is a stream, and
        a value that is not there yet is a hole in it rather than a reason to hold it back.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/send-the-page-before-the-data-lands')}>Streaming HTML</a> ·
        <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="5. The document closes">
        <p>The document closes as soon as the walk is done. A value still in flight at that point is
        left as a hole, and nothing is written after the markup to fill it. The browser's own call
        for that value fills the hole once the page has taken over, and the buffer below answers
        that call.</p>

        <p>Nothing is written after the markup to fill a hole. The one script the page carries is
        the early-call script in the head, and it starts requests rather than writing values.</p>

        <p><strong>The cost is a client with no script.</strong> A hole stays empty for it, because
        the script does the filling. A page that must work without one is a page whose values have
        to be there when the walk passes them, which is the server's business rather than the
        template's.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="6. The browser takes over">
        <p>Markers and the bundle. Not a serialized tree to diff, and not a copy of your data in the
        document — the client reruns the same setup the server ran: every
        <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, every <code>reactive</code> call, every body, again.</p>

        <p>That sounds like paying twice, and for the loads it is not. <code>reactive(() =&gt;
        getInvoice(…))</code> runs a second time in the browser and makes the same call — one the
        page <strong>already started</strong> while it was still arriving. Once setup has run, the
        render writes one small script into the head, and it sends each call the page made as its
        own request. The bundle is still downloading at that point, so by the time it runs, the
        answers are usually there, and hydration takes them without asking again.</p>

        <p>The render <strong>buffered what it fetched</strong>, so each of those requests is
        answered from the buffer rather than from the database. The handler runs once. The request
        still shows in the network panel like any other, with its address, its status and a
        <code>Seeded</code> entry under <code>server-timing</code> saying it was answered from the
        render's run and how long the handler took.</p>

        <p>That saves the <strong>handler's work</strong> and a round trip after the bundle. On a
        slow connection the bundle is the long wait, and the calls ride inside it rather than after
        it.</p>

        <p>Binding happens against the nodes that are already there. A marker whose content does not
        match rebuilds its own block, not the page.</p>

        <p><strong>A body that is expensive and is not a load is genuinely paid twice</strong> —
        there is nothing to seed for a pure computation. That is the case that belongs behind a
        handler, where it runs on the server and arrives as data.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="7. The next navigation">
        <p>Once the page has hydrated, the browser does the rendering. A navigation fetches what the
        next page reads rather than a new document, and the layouts a route shares with the one
        before it are never rebuilt — a header with an open menu in it stays exactly as it was.</p>

        <p>A navigation the page already standing matches — another query, or another
        <code>[id]</code> of the same route — rebuilds nothing at all. <code>route.url</code> and
        <code>route.params</code> move, and what reads them follows:
        <code>{'reactive(() => getInvoice({ id: route.params.id }))'}</code> loads the next invoice
        while a search box keeps its focus and the scroll stays where it was (30.23, 30.25).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/animate-from-one-page-to-the-next')}>View transitions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The wire carries the same seven steps">
        <p><code>/invoices/42</code>, against the page in step 4. This actually leaves the
        server.</p>

        <p><strong>Steps 1–4, the head.</strong> Nothing was awaited to produce it. Two of its
        elements are abide's: the seed manifest, naming the calls this render is making, and the
        bootstrap that issues them.</p>

        <Code language="html" caption="browser" code={`<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<script type="application/json" data-abide-seed>
[["GET","/invoices/42/getInvoice",{}]]
</script>
<script>/* issues each call, stashes each Promise under its key */</script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/_abide/app.7f3c9a1e.css">
</head>
<body>`} />

        <p>The manifest is <code>(method, address, args)</code> — what to fetch, never what was
        fetched. So the browser has the request in flight before the bundle exists, and before the
        body it will fill has been written.</p>

        <p><strong>Step 4, the markup.</strong> The row has not landed, so each read leaves a sink:
        a <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> holding the hole's id, and the filler script beside it.</p>

        <Code language="html" caption="browser" code={`<h1>Invoice
<template data-abide-sink="3"></template><script>/* fill 3 */</script></h1>
<p><template data-abide-sink="4"></template><script>/* fill 4 */</script>
due <template data-abide-sink="5"></template><script>/* fill 5 */</script>
</p>`} />

        <p>Those filler scripts are <strong>byte-identical</strong> — every one of them, in every
        document abide serves. Each reads the <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> next to it, which is
        where everything specific to that hole lives. That lets one build-time hash cover them all,
        and keeps a per-request nonce out of the head.</p>

        <p><strong>Step 5, a fill.</strong> Written at the current end of the document, finding the
        hole by id. Where an element genuinely cannot go, the id rides on the owning element instead
        and the filler writes one property.</p>

        <Code language="html" caption="browser" code={`<template data-abide-sink="3" data-abide-fill>42</template>
<script>/* fill 3 */</script>`} />

        <p><strong>Step 6.</strong> The bundle loads and reruns the setup. <code>getInvoice</code>
        is called again and answered from the buffer, and binding happens against the nodes
        above.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The directory a file is in decides where it runs">
        <p><code>src/</code> is the app, and every file in it runs on both sides unless its
        directory says otherwise. Group it by subject — <code>src/billing/page.abide</code>,
        <code>src/billing/server</code>, <code>src/billing/ui</code> — and the build enforces the
        split wherever the directories sit.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Directory</th><th>What is in it</th><th>Reached by</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/**/</code></td><td>addresses: pages, layouts, and the handlers a <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> exports</td><td>anything; the browser gets a handler's client</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>**/server/**</code></td><td>code the browser never loads</td><td>the server alone; a page importing it is a compile error</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>#src/…</code> is the one import alias, mapping onto <code>src/</code>. Two files
        are addresses rather than names, and both are optional:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/layout.abide</code></td><td>the root layout: the document and the chrome every page shares, and the app's own <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/run-code-at-start-and-stop')}>Lifecycle</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/templates')}>Templates</a> — the markup half, in full</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values')}>Reactive values</a> — what a read actually returns</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages')}>Pages and navigation</a> — routes, layouts and error
            pages</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
